import mundane theorem    stdlib/reasoning/modus-ponens

theorem cases-by-implication
  metamath    jao
  note        If a = 0 gives c = 0 and b = 0 gives c = 0, then whichever of
              the two holds, c = 0 follows.
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  then if (if a = 0 then c = 0) then if (if b = 0 then c = 0) then if (a = 0 or b = 0) then c = 0

1.  If (if a = 0 then c = 0) then if (if b = 0 then c = 0) then if (a = 0 or b = 0) then c = 0.
    proof
    assume if a = 0 then c = 0                                        (K1)

    1.1.  If (if b = 0 then c = 0) then if (a = 0 or b = 0) then c = 0.
          proof
          assume if b = 0 then c = 0                                  (K2)

          1.1.1.  If (a = 0 or b = 0) then c = 0.
                  proof
                  assume a = 0 or b = 0                               (K3)

                  1.1.1.1.  c = 0
                            cases, from K3

                            case
                            assume a = 0                              (C1)

                            1.1.1.1.1.  c = 0
                                        mun:modus-ponens, from C1, K1

                            case
                            assume b = 0                              (C2)

                            1.1.1.1.2.  c = 0
                                        mun:modus-ponens, from C2, K2
